expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["zeros on its diagonal","-1"],"must":["Q\\^\\{-1\\} = Q","triangular","\\\\det M = 4"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 1**

$M$ is block upper triangular:

$$M = \begin{pmatrix} A & B \\ 0 & D \end{pmatrix}, \quad A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix}, \quad D = \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 6 \\ 7 & 8 \end{pmatrix}$$

with $B$ any $2 \times 2$ block. Find $\det M$.

<solution>For a block triangular matrix, det M = det A · det D = (4 − 6)(40 − 42) = (−2)(−2) = 4. B does not affect it.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import numpy as np
A = np.array([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]]); D = np.array([[5.0, 6.0], [7.0, 8.0]])
for B in (np.zeros((2, 2)), np.array([[9.0, -4.0], [2.0, 7.0]])):
    M = np.block([[A, B], [np.zeros((2, 2)), D]])
    assert abs(np.linalg.det(M) - 4) < 1e-9
</check>

<ask type="problem">$\det M$, with the rule you used.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
det A * det D = (4-6)(40-42) = (-2)(-2) = 4. B doesn't come into it
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"det A * det D" is the rule for a block triangular matrix, and "B doesn't come into it" is right: $(-2)(-2) = 4$.</grade>

**Problem 2 · a little harder**

$N$ is block diagonal:

$$N = \begin{pmatrix} P & 0 \\ 0 & Q \end{pmatrix}, \quad P = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad Q = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$$

Find $N^{-1}$, or say why it does not exist.

<solution>Invert block by block. det P = 1, so P⁻¹ = [[1, −1], [−1, 2]]. det Q = −1 and QQ = I, so Q⁻¹ = Q. N⁻¹ is block diagonal with blocks [[1, −1], [−1, 2]] and [[0, 1], [1, 0]].</solution>
<check lang="python">
import numpy as np
P = np.array([[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0]]); Q = np.array([[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]])
Z = np.zeros((2, 2))
N = np.block([[P, Z], [Z, Q]])
Ninv = np.block([[np.array([[1.0, -1.0], [-1.0, 2.0]]), Z], [Z, Q]])
assert np.allclose(N @ Ninv, np.eye(4))
</check>

<ask type="problem">$N^{-1}$ as blocks, or the reason there is none.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
Block diagonal so you invert each block. P^-1 = [[1,-1],[-1,2]]. But Q has zeros on its diagonal so Q is singular, which means N has no inverse.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.5">"invert each block" is the right rule and "P^-1 = [[1,-1],[-1,2]]" is right. "Q has zeros on its diagonal so Q is singular" is the step that fails: $\det Q = 0 \cdot 0 - 1 \cdot 1 = -1$, which is not zero. $Q$ swaps the two coordinates, so $Q^{-1} = Q$, and $N^{-1}$ exists: block diagonal with blocks $P^{-1}$ and $Q$.</grade>

<card>Does a zero on the diagonal make a matrix singular? :: No. A matrix is singular when its determinant is 0: [[0, 1], [1, 0]] has determinant $-1$ and is its own inverse. A zero on the diagonal settles it only for a triangular matrix.</card>

<note>A zero on the diagonal does not make a matrix singular; the determinant decides. $\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ has determinant $-1$ and is its own inverse.</note>

<summary>**What came back:** the block rules. The determinant of a block triangular matrix is the product of the determinants of its diagonal blocks ($\det M = 4$, whatever $B$ is), and a block diagonal matrix is inverted block by block.

**Still shaky:** judging whether a matrix is singular by looking at its diagonal. That works only for a triangular matrix; otherwise compute the determinant. $Q$ has zeros on its diagonal, determinant $-1$, and is its own inverse.

**Next:** a short pass on tests for a singular matrix, then multiplying block matrices that have off-diagonal blocks.</summary>
@@ verify
import numpy as np
A = np.array([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]]); D = np.array([[5.0, 6.0], [7.0, 8.0]])
assert round(np.linalg.det(A)) == -2 == 4 - 6 and round(np.linalg.det(D)) == -2 == 40 - 42
P = np.array([[2, 1], [1, 1]]); Q = np.array([[0, 1], [1, 0]])
Pinv = np.array([[1, -1], [-1, 2]])
assert (P @ Pinv).tolist() == [[1, 0], [0, 1]]                    # the learner's P^-1 is right
assert 0 * 0 - 1 * 1 == -1 and round(np.linalg.det(Q)) == -1      # Q is not singular
assert (Q @ Q).tolist() == [[1, 0], [0, 1]]                       # Q is its own inverse
assert (Q @ [3, 8]).tolist() == [8, 3]                            # it swaps the two coordinates
Z = np.zeros((2, 2), dtype=int)
N = np.block([[P, Z], [Z, Q]]); Ninv = np.block([[Pinv, Z], [Z, Q]])
assert (N @ Ninv).tolist() == np.eye(4, dtype=int).tolist()
# a triangular matrix with a zero on its diagonal is singular (the card's last sentence)
T = np.array([[2.0, 5.0], [0.0, 0.0]])
assert abs(np.linalg.det(T)) < 1e-12
